# PART V. TECHNIQUE-INTEGRATION REQUIREMENTS

## 24. Wraytracing must be visibly ambitious

Give Wraytracing enough space to operate beyond simple illumination.

Include several meaningful instances of high-resolution perceptual rendering across the passage. These should include, where physically applicable:

- Relationships among at least two or three colours rather than isolated colour labels.
- A tonal or chromatic transition that helps establish form, distance, atmosphere, or mood.
- A reflection or shadow whose appearance depends on a specific surface, angle, obstruction, or movement.
- A material whose physical properties influence its visible appearance.
- A change in the perceptual field as the character or another object moves.
- At least one description where the arrangement of colour, tone, texture, depth, and contrast creates an emotional or poetic effect without naming the emotion directly.

Do not force every item into one passage. Distribute them where the environment and action justify them.

The important requirement is not the number of visual details. It is the **precision and interdependence of the rendered details**.

A description should help the reader perceive a particular arrangement, not merely recognize that the writer knows many visual adjectives.

If you use metaphor, preserve the underlying perceptual reality. The metaphor should intensify or transform the reader's experience of the observed scene, not replace the scene with decorative abstraction.

## 25. Integrate sound and physical sensation

Wraytracing must not monopolize the sensory field.

Use sound, vibration, temperature, pressure, movement, texture, bodily orientation, or smell when relevant.

Tie sensory information to identifiable sources or conditions whenever possible. A sound should have a plausible origin, a relationship to distance or obstruction, and an effect on what the character notices or does.

Do not mention every sense merely to demonstrate completeness.

## 26. Make the reader perform meaningful inferences

Include at least one moment when the reader can form a plausible interpretation before the character fully understands the situation, or when the character's initial interpretation differs from what later information supports.

The inference must be fair. Its supporting evidence should exist in the scene.

Use Subtext & Lacunae for indirect meaning or unresolved interpretation, and Subtext & Ellipsis for omission or compression. Keep their functions distinct.

The scene may preserve some uncertainty at the end, but the immediate practical sequence must remain understandable.

## 27. Use one or two meaningful repetitions

Develop one primary recurring element and, if useful, one secondary recurrence.

A repeated element may change from background information to evidence, from a neutral sound to an anticipated signal, or from a seemingly decorative image to a marker of changed understanding.

Do not make every colour, sound, object, and phrase a symbol. The environment is allowed to contain ordinary things.

## 28. Let form respond to events

Use Cadence Engine and Glyphcraft to shape the reading experience.

Include variation in sentence movement and paragraph rhythm. Use fragmentation or isolation only where the scene's timing, attention, or interpretation justifies it.

A quiet observation may require a sustained sentence. A physical interruption may require a sudden break. A revelation may require an unexpectedly plain statement rather than a flourish.

These are possible decisions, not fixed rules.

## 29. Preserve material and causal reality

The environment must behave as a place rather than as a collection of metaphors.

Objects occupy positions. Surfaces have properties. People move with physical constraints. Sounds originate somewhere. Reflections depend on viewing conditions. Actions produce consequences.

The scene may use poetic language to transform the reader's experience of these facts, but poetic language does not excuse physical incoherence.

When a description and an action conflict, repair the conflict rather than asking the reader to admire the sentence.

